  "narrative": {
    "intro": "New Jersey recorded $8.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $6.1B, making defense the dominant buyer. Obligations fell 4.1% year over year, while the state recorded 395,587 awards with an average award size of $22.6K. Agency buying beyond Defense includes the Department of Veterans Affairs at $855M, the Department of Homeland Security at $646M, and the Department of Transportation at $347M. Contractors in New Jersey won large shares of the total: LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION received $2.9B, ASRC FEDERAL FACILITIES LOGISTICS, LLC received $554M, and WHITING-TURNER CONTRACTING COMPANY, THE received $400M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry category at $2.3B, followed by Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $1.1B. The fiscal totals shown are partial. The note says all figures are contract obligations and that current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are the biggest buyers in New Jersey?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the biggest buyer at $6.1B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs follows at $855M and the Department of Homeland Security follows at $646M. The Department of Transportation, Department of Health and Human Services, and Department of Energy round out the top six at $347M, $316M, and $174M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the most federal dollars in New Jersey?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences leads industries at $2.3B. Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing is next at $1.1B. LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION is the largest vendor at $2.9B, with ASRC FEDERAL FACILITIES LOGISTICS, LLC at $554M and WHITING-TURNER CONTRACTING COMPANY, THE at $400M."
      }
